diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk claim — that router16 is down, has lost its OSPF neighbors, and has cut off Campus 1 (172.16.10/20/30.0/24) — is not supported by any live evidence. A full four-phase diagnostic sweep found no fault.

**Affected component:** None. All subsystems are nominal.

**Mechanism (why the claim does not hold):** Router16 is running, its FRR ospfd daemon is active, and it holds two Full OSPF adjacencies: one on bond0 (10.2.2.1/24) toward mswitch0, and one on eth2 (172.30.1.1/24) toward router7. The mswitch0 multilayer switch correctly redistributes the three Campus 1 subnets (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24) into OSPF area 0 through its br0 interface, which peers with router16 at Full adjacency. All nine routers carry a consistent 18-route OSPF table, indicating complete convergence. End-to-end reachability tests (Campus 1 hosts → external hosts and vice versa) all returned 0% packet loss.

**Localization:** No device or interface is implicated. infra_sweep flagged zero devices, l2_snapshot found zero duplicate MACs, pressure_sweep found zero resource anomalies, and the OSPF snapshot confirms every router — including router16 — is fully adjacent.

**Root cause:** There is no current fault. The reported outage either resolved on its own before this investigation began, was never present, or was a misidentification. No flap process, no carrier-down history, and no stale OSPF adjacency trace was found.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the symptom recurs, collect OSPF neighbor logs (`show ip ospf neighbor`) and interface carrier counters from router16 at the time of the event to establish a timeline.
